// Overrides for the AI, on top of ai.zrd, aiv*.zrd and net_NN.zrd.
// **Empty is correct**: the game's own reader data is primary (DESIGN §5.3).
//
// The split this file draws: the game states
// every parameter of *what* its AI does and all of it is read:
//
//   activate_rad, attack_rad, attack_dwell, not_pursuit_dwell,
//   pursuit_params/pursuit_range, return_range, hide_times, attack_strategy,
//   activate_buddy, path_width and the waypoint graph   (net_NN.zrd)
//   HEALTH, PARTS, WEAPON/{NAME,AMMO,FIRE_RATE,FIRE_LIMITS,DETECTION_RANGE,
//   BASE_MOVES,FIRE_DWELL,DAMAGE_MODIFIER}, LOS, MSL_LOCK, TARGETS,
//   DESTROY_ANIM, DEACTIVATE, EFFECT                    (ai.zrd)
//   activation, pursuit_range, not_pursuit_dwell, return_range, the weapon
//   row's engagement range                              (vehicle.zrd)
//
// None of those belongs here. What belongs here is what *our* steering
// invented, because the original has no equivalent: how often a brain
// re-perceives, how far it leads, how badly it aims, how far its whiskers
// reach. Each is marked `// approximation` at its definition in
// crates/bft-sim/src/ai/tuning.rs, and each is settable here:
//
//   (
//       reaction_ticks: Some(6),   // ticks between perception passes
//       lead_factor:    Some(0.85),// 1.0 is a perfect interception solution
//       aim_error_deg:  Some(2.0), // max angular error, not a std deviation
//       steering: Some((
//           los_lost_ticks:    Some(3),     // passes of no LOS before the
//                                           // target is dropped; the
//                                           // *seconds* are the data's
//                                           // not_pursuit_dwell
//           arrive_radius:     Some(10.0),  // m; from path_width
//           whisker_length:    Some(18.0),  // m
//           whisker_angle_deg: Some(35.0),
//           whisker_height:    Some(1.0),   // m above the hull origin
//           steer_gain:        Some(4.0),
//           strafe_period:     Some(3.0),   // s per back_forth leg
//           fire_cone_deg:     Some(8.0),   // must exceed aim_error_deg
//           turret_yaw_rate:   Some(1.6),   // rad/s, ai.zrd turrets
//           turret_pitch_rate: Some(1.0),
//           reverse_angle_deg: Some(110.0), // past this the goal is behind
//           reverse_max_distance: Some(25.0), // m; nearer, back up to it;
//                                           // further, turn round forwards
//           min_engage_speed:  Some(0.15),  // throttle floor
//           activation_scale:  Some(1.0),   // factor on every net's
//                                           // activate_rad
//           roam_range:        Some(400.0), // m; a sleeping unit walks its
//                                           // net while the player is this
//                                           // near, without hunting; 0 parks
//       )),
//       // Per vehicle.zrd kind. Layered over the values above.
//       by_kind: {
//           "dron": (lead_factor: Some(1.0), no_whiskers: Some(true)),
//       },
//   )
//
// The two maps below are an **escape hatch, not a tuning surface**. One
// hand-authored net out of 470, or one ai.zrd entry out of 130, may turn out
// wrong in play; correcting it here beats editing game data we do not ship.
// One file serves all 13 levels, so a net or glob the current mission does
// not have is warned about and the game keeps playing.
//
//   nets:    { 1: (activate_rad: Some(50.0), pursuit: Some((10.0, 20.0))) },
//   turrets: { "tur_1**": (health: Some(99.0), fire_interval: Some(0.5)) },
//
// An unknown field is a parse error, not a silent no-op, so a typo is
// reported rather than ignored.
//
// The dev panel's "AI tuning" tab edits the mission-wide knobs live and
// saves them here: only values that differ from the compiled defaults are
// written, and this header and the sections below are kept. When a value
// changes, note why in this header.
(
    by_kind: {},
    nets: {},
    turrets: {},
)
